# Worklane visual quality

Worklane should feel like a premium studio operating system: structured, dense, polished, and expressive. Do not flatten it into a generic minimal SaaS layout.

Hierarchy means the important thing is one level stronger. It does not mean everything else gets removed or washed out.

## Keep structure

Cards, borders, shadows, badges, and surfaces are tools. Use them at different weights:

- Dominant — the object (name, title, main number)
- Primary — project, client, current state
- Secondary — owner, due date, supporting context
- Muted — extra metadata

A rich component is correct when those weights are different. Do not strip badges, metric surfaces, or row columns just to make a screen quieter.

## Shared pieces

- Wide records use `DetailedRow`: identity, a labeled context column, a labeled signal column, then actions. Do not drop billed, collected, tracking, signatures, or project names to make the row shorter.
- Compact scans use `EntityRow` when the object really is one line.
- Figures use `MetricGroup` / `SummaryStat`. Each figure keeps its own surface. Mark one figure stronger; do not shrink the rest into plain text.
- Board cards keep a project badge and priority, with the task title as the strongest line.
- Secondary actions stay outline or tonal. Ghost is for rare or icon actions.
- Empty states stay compact and still look designed.

## Before changing a component

Name the actual problem: hierarchy, layout, density, alignment, interaction, styling, or information architecture. Fix that. Do not start by deleting visual elements.
